1 CONT In the 1850 Census he is listed as 16 years old.
1 CONT
1 CONT "Rev CALEB S. TAYLOR.  Mr Taylor is a native of Marion county, born September 26, 1833.  He is the son of Caleb Taylor, deceased.  During his boyhood he attended the district schools of his neighborhood, where he acquired a deep thirst for knowled
1 CONC ge.  Much of his time was employed on the farm, and he had to contend with the disadvantages of life in a new country.  He attended college at Palmyra, and the William Jewell College at Liberty, Clay county, and in 1859 and 1860 he was at school i
1 CONC n Kentucky.  August 1, 1861, Mr. Taylor was married to Miss Lucinda A Smoot, daughter of Josiah and Harriet (Whittaker) Smoot, the former of Fauquier county, and the latter of Culpepper county, Virginia.  Mrs Taylor is a native of Marion county, b
1 CONC orn January 25, 1840.  August 1, 1862, Mr. Taylor enlisted in the Confederate service under Capt. Brown.  He was however out only a few weeks, the company disbanding soon after the engagement at Kirksville.  Mr Taylor was licensed to preach in Jan
1 CONC uary, 1857, and had engaged in this calling while attending school, and in 1867 he was ordained a minister, and has since engaged almost continually in his work, being an earnest and faithful laborer in the cause of Christianity.  Mr. and Mrs. Tay
1 CONC lor have four children: Allyn O., born March 10, 1862l Caleb S. born July 19, 1863; Leander W., born April 18, 1866, and Josiah S. born December 3, 1867."--Holcombe. op. cit., pp. 798-799.
0 @N827@ NOTE
1 CONC "Nellie Virginia Young, daughter of Clifton B. and Rose Pulliam Young was born Feb 17, 1891 and died Sept. 26, 1916.
1 CONT "The funeral sermon was preached at Bethel Baptist Church by Rev W.M. Alderton, and interment was in Bethel Cemetery where lie four generations of her ancestors.
1 CONT "Much of her school life was spent in this city where her home was with her grandmother, Mrs Rebecca Pulliam.
1 CONT "To few, of her short years, has it been given to win and hold the esteem and love of so many friends both young and old, who were attracted by her quiet, kindly, tactful manner of dealing with those with whom she was associated.
1 CONT "During months of intense suffering, which she bore with quiet courage, hosts of friends, whom she had made in happier days, did all for her that human hands and love could do.
1 CONT "Life was sweet to her and she desired to live but she said repeatedly, 'I am not afraid to die, I am trusting Jesus.'
1 CONT "Death to her was not the end of a toilsome journey, nor the laying down of a crushing load, but the happy homegoing to a land where there shall be no more pain and where the inhabitants shall no more say, I am sick.
1 CONT "We are happier because she has lived and still lives."  -- presumably from the Palmyra Spectator.
0 @N828@ NOTE
1 CONC The name Clifton B. Young is given in the obituary of Nellie Virginia Young.   Since his daughter lived with his wife's mother, one wonders what became of Cliff and his wife?
0 @N829@ NOTE
1 CONC "W.L. Cooper Funeral Conducted
1 CONT "Services for Wilson L. Cooper, 50 of 103 Blackburn Drive, partner in the Cooper, Lowe and Jackson Company insurance agency were held at 2 p.m. today at St. Bartholomew's Episcopal Church.  The Rev. Robert Hayden, rector there, officiated.
1 CONT "Burial was in Woodlawn Memorial Park, Finley Dorris & Charlton Funeral Home was in charge.
1 CONT "Mr Cooper died at 4 p.m. in St. Thomas Hospital after being ill 18 months.  Born and reared in Nashville, he was a son of Robert Emmett and Annie Wilson Cooper.  He was educated at Peabody Demonstration School.  Mr. Cooper, formerly operated th
1 CONC e Bill Cooper Insurance Agency.  He Married Anne Harrison 11 years ago.  She survives.
1 CONT "Mr. Cooper was a director of Goodwill Industries Inc., past president of the Insurors of Nashville, past treasurer of the Insurors of Tennessee and a member of the Sertoma Club.  He was a member of St. Bartholomew's Episcopal Church.
1 CONT "Other survivors are two daughters, Virginia Cooper and Lloyd [sic] Cooper; a son, Robert Emmitt Cooper, and three sisters, Mrs. Eugene Taylor and Mrs Margaret Walker, both of Nashville and Mrs. Irma Waddell of Tucson." -- The Nashville Banner, Mo
1 CONC n., Jan. 21, 1963, p. 6
1 CONT
1 CONT I believe that I met Bill and his wife during one of our trips to Nashville.  At that time, he was a paraplegic possibly from an accident.  The two girls were adopted twins, of which one was blind.  I remember thinking what a lot Mrs. Cooper had o
1 CONC n her plate with the care of husband and children. -- LKL
