1 CONT 1807  JOHN JOHNS sold 220 acres to Samuel Johns
1 CONT
1 CONT 1813-14  JOHN JOHNS' residence was on the Buckingham Road, adjacent to Glo ver Johns.
1 CONT
1 CONT 1815  JOHN JOHNS owned 459 acres of land adjacent to Samuel Johns and Jam es Gilliam
1 CONT
1 CONT 1815  JOHN JOHNS sold 100 acres to Peter Francisco
1 CONT
1 CONT 1816  The will of JOHN JOHNS gave 459 acres adjacent to Samuel Johns to Jo hn Johns, Jr.
1 CONT
1 CONT In November 2002, I received an e-mail from Russ Peck of Nashville, TN. Ru ss is a descendant of Mary "Polly" Johns Thompson, daughter of Elizabeth G over and JOHN JOHNS.  In Russ' possession were documents which had pass ed down and had been pres
1 CONC erved by the ancestors of Mary Johns Thompson. In cluded in these documents was a copy of the Last Will and Testament of Jo hn Johns.  This document is of immense importance, as the copy placed in to probate in 1815  was destroyed when the Bucking
1 CONC ham County court house b urned in 1869. Until now, John Johns' LWT was considered a lost documen t.  My sincere thanks to Russ and his father, Earsel Elliott Peck, for sha ring their family papers and for allowing me to post them. I have transcr
1 CONC i bed the Last Will and Testament from a copy of the original.  (Bob Newsom e, December 2002)
1 CONT
1 CONT The Last Will and Testament of JOHN JOHNS was signed on 29 April 1815 a nd was placed into probate in Buckingham Co., Virginia on 12 June 1815.
1 CONT
1 CONT I, JOHN JOHNS of Buckingham County, do hereby make my last will and testam ent in the manner and form following, that is to say:
1 CONT
1 CONT 1st: I desire that all my past debts should be paid.
1 CONT
1 CONT 2nd: I give to my son John Johns all my tract of land whereon I now liv e, lying in the county of Buckingham, except a small parcel of land lyi ng on the east side of the road leading to Williams Jones' mill, adjoini ng the New Store tract, whic
1 CONC h small parcel of land I give to my son-in-l aw William Thompson.
1 CONT
1 CONT 3rd: I give to my son Anthony Johns the tract of land whereon my son Jo hn Johns now lives, to be laid off on the north and south sides of the ma in road, containing two hundred and sixteen acres, the title to be made fr om John Johns and Willia
1 CONC m Thompson. The land to be laid off, beginni ng at the upper end, or brickyard, and coming down both sides of the ro ad until you to get ____ above named.
1 CONT
1 CONT 4th: I give to my two daughters Martha Cock and Sarah Crawford one hundr ed pounds each, to be raised from the sale of part of my estate.
1 CONT
1 CONT 5th: I give to my daughter Judith Cock two negroes (to wit) Sellar and Jan e.
1 CONT
1 CONT 6th: I give to my daughter Elizabeth Wood, Phebey and her child Eda, al so a feather bed and furniture.
1 CONT
1 CONT 7th: I give to my son-in-law William Thompson two hundred and sixty six po unds, which I have paid toward the purchase of the New Store tract of la nd from Peter Francisco. Also I give to him all claim, either in bon ds or open account, which I ha
1 CONC ve against him.
1 CONT
1 CONT 8th: I give in trust to my son Glover Johns and John Gilliam, for the bene fit of my daughter Polly Thompson and the heirs of her body, during her na tural life, the following negroes, to wit, Biddy, Darcus, Hannah, Sam, To m, Jane and John. It'
1 CONC s my wish also after the decease of my daughter Pol ly Thompson, if she dies before William Thompson, that the above trust a nd negroes should be continued for the benefit of William Thompson duri ng his natural life.  I also give to my daughter P
1 CONC olly Thompson one feath er bed and furniture.
1 CONT
1 CONT 9th: I give to my daughter Nancy Johns,  six negroes, to wit, Flora, Bo b, Sarah, Lucy, Paulina, and Abba, Flora's mother. Also two feather beds a nd furniture of her own choice and all the sheets, ____,  curtains, tab le clothes, which she may cl
1 CONC aim as her own manufacture. The furniture whi ch has generally set in her room, including half dozen Windsor chairs, al so the gig and choice of my ____, also all my silver plate to be divided w ith her sisters Polly Thompson and Elizabeth Wood.
1 CONT
1 CONT 10th: I give to my son Gover Johns, two negroes, Frank and Joe.
1 CONT
1 CONT 11th: I give to my son Samuel Johns, one negro, Allie, a feather bed and f urniture.
1 CONT
1 CONT 12th: I give to my son John Johns one negro: Savory.
1 CONT
1 CONT 13th: I give to my granddaughter Elizabeth Thompson one negro boy, Carter.
1 CONT
1 CONT 14th: I give to my grandson Edmund G. Johns, son of William Johns, two hun dred dollars, and to my son William Johns, five dollars.
1 CONT
1 CONT 15th: I give to my son Glover Johns and Samuel Johns and my daughter Pol ly Thompson, four negroes, Fordy, Sam, Nancy and Abba, to be divided as th ey may agree. not parting man and wife, my will and desire is the above ne groes to be well treate
1 CONC d during life.
1 CONT
1 CONT 16th: The amount of the bond executed by William Thompson and John Joh ns to Lewis Wilkinson, now in the possession of Samuel Johns, to be paid o ut of my estate, it being a balance due from me for the purchase of la nd I gave to my son Anthony Jo
1 CONC hns.
1 CONT
1 CONT 17th: The residue of my estate I wish sold, debts and monied legacies ____ ___. The balance of my estate ___ provided ____ away to be divided equal ly between my three daughters, Nancy Johns, Polly Thompson and Elizabeth W ood.
1 CONT
1 CONT 18th: If the residue of my estate should not be sufficient to pay my deb ts and specific money legacies, my will and desire is that each legatee sh ould bear their proportionable part, except my daughter Judith Cock.
1 CONT
1 CONT 19th: The present crop I desire to be finished, the hands and other ____ t hing to finish the crop to remain until the same is completed in due time.
1 CONT
1 CONT 20th: And lastly. I do hereby constitute and appoint my three sons, Glove r, Anthony and John Johns and my sons-in-law William Thompson and Edmund W ood executors of this my last will and testament, hereby revoking all t he other or former wills o
1 CONC r testaments by me heretofore made. In witness w hereof I have hereon to set my hand and affixed my seal, this 29th d ay of April 1815.
1 CONT
1 CONT JOHN JOHNS (Seal)
1 CONT
1 CONT Signed and sealed in the presence of:
1 CONT John S. Mills
1 CONT P. H. Anderson
1 CONT Thomas C. Armstrong
1 CONT
1 CONT At a court held for Buckingham County the 12th day of June 1815:
1 CONT
1 CONT This will was proved by the oaths of John S. Mills and P. H. Anderson, t wo of the witnesses and sworn to by Glover Johns one of the executors ther ein named and is ordered to be recorded. And on the motion of the said exe cutor who enters into an
1 CONC d acknowledges a bond with security according to l aw, certificate is granted him for attaining a probate thereof in due form .
1 CONT
1 CONT Teste: Rolfe Eldridge, C. C.
