1 CONC "Born about 1756 in Frederick County, Virginia (in 1840, Kentucky Census, Jacob states he was 84 years of age--See Collin's History of Kentucky, page 8).  About the year 1762 he came with his parents from Frederick County to Montgomery County, Vir
1 CONC ginia, where he grew to manhood.  In March 1777, he enlisted at Montgomery Court House, Virginia and served for more than a year under Capt. Joseph Crockett in the 7th Virginia Regiment and in Colonel William Heth's Regiment in the Revolutionary W
1 CONC ar.  He was in the battle of Monmouth, New Jersey (fought June 28, 1778).  Following this service in the army, he migrated to Kentucky where on November 10, 1786, he married Rachel English in Lincoln County.  He lived for a time in Madison Count
1 CONC y and finally settled in Rockcastle County, about 1825, where he lived on a large plantation near Boone, Kentucky, until his death June 6, 1849, aged about 93 years.  From the year 1784 to 1800, Jacob accumulated about two thousand acres of land
1 CONC , some of this, located near Boone, Kentucky, is still in possession of his descendants.  He was buried on his farm near Boone.  He was allowed a Revolutionary War soldier's pension on his application executed June 17, 1819 (pension claim W-2019
1 CONC ) at which time he was a resident of Madison County, Kentucky.
1 CONT "Jacob Stephens participated in a Militia or volunteer engagement at the Blue Lick, near Carlisle, Kentucky.  This place has been made a State Park, and a thirty foot marble monument has been erected to the memory of those who were mur-"  [photoco
1 CONC pied page from JET ends here] -- Stephens, Dan V. Stephens Family Genealogies,  p. unpaged [6]
0 @N915@ NOTE
1 CONC "Born in Frederick County, Virginia about 1755, and came with his father to Montgomery County, Virginia about 1767.  In 1775, he enlisted in the army and saw service in the Revolutionary War, as a private and as a corporal (See Claim S 7689 00 Cer
1 CONC t. 16465, Veterans Bureau, Washington, D.C.).  After the war, he and his brother, Joseph Stephens went to Wythe County where in 1787, he married Joanna Herbert and located on a 600 acre tract of land, on Little Reed Island Creek, which he acquire
1 CONC d August 17, 1793.  He built for his residence, a small two room log house, in which he lived until he could build a larger one, which he built about the year 1795, the ruins of which are still standing, and lived there until his death May 3, 1847
1 CONC , at over 90 years of age.  His will which is recorded in the Clerk's Office of Carroll County, Virgina, WB 1 p. 148, leaves his estate to his children."  8 children are listed --  Stephens, Dan V.  Stephens Family Genealogies,  pp. unpaged [5&6]
