1 CONC He died in 1705 when his ship, the Privateer, "The Castle Del Key,"  130 tons, 18 guns, was wrecked on east bank of Sandy Hook on the 19th of December.  Mr Cortelyou of the Narrows failed in his rescue (The Boston News Letter of Dec. 16-24, 1705
1 CONC , No. 88, New Jersey Archives, First series, Vol. XI, p 4; The Pirates' who's who by Phillip Gosse.)
1 CONT
1 CONT Will was dated 12/2/1704; proved Aug 26, 1708. -- Alma Cramer Notes.
1 CONT
1 CONT Will of Otto Van Tuyl of New York dated Dec 2, 1704, Proved Aug 26, 1708
1 CONT "I, Otto van Tuyl of New York, merchant....leave to my eldest son Dirck Van Tuyl, 6 shillings when of age or married.  All rest of my real estate, real and personal, I leave to my wife Margaret, during widowhood....If she marries, then she shall d
1 CONC eliver to my children Dirck, Jan and Anna, what she shall have undisposed of.  If my children should die, then one-half of estate to go to my brothers, Aert, Abraham and Isaac Van Tuyl, and to Elizabeth, wife of W,. Pell; Antie, wife of Corneliu
1 CONC s Van der Vanter.  And one-half to my wife's mother Elizabeth, wife of Joris Burger;...I make my brother-in-law, Cornelius Van der Venter [sic.] and Moses Gilbert, guardians of my children under age..." -- New York Historical Society, Vol II, Abst
1 CONC ract of wills, p. 9.
1 CONT
1 CONT 1705 -- "New York, Dec. 24 -- On the 19th inst. the Private Ship of War call'd The Castel Del Key of 130 Tons, 18 Guns, Capt. Otto Van Tuyle, Commander Sailed from Jackques Bay (about ten miles from hence) and going down towards Sandy Hook with a
1 CONC n easy Gale of Wind, she struck upon the East bank and stuck there; They sent some of their men on Shoar in their canoo for boats to assist then [them?], but that night a hard Gale of Wind Sprung up between W. and N.W. and Froze very hard, the Shi
1 CONC p began to fill with water.
1 CONT
1 CONT "A sloop and large Boat were sent down, but it was Freezing and blowing so hard, they would not venture to relieve them, for fear of running the same fate of being a ground and so froze and drowned; The next Morning the Gale continued hard all day
1 CONC , and the men were all alive upon the Deck and in the Shrouds; The Sea beating over them; And on Fryday Morning the Wind and all the rest being Froze and Drownd, there was 145 men on board when she sailed, who all perished but 13 and 132 dyed in t
1 CONC his deplorable manner.  Here are widows Lamenting the loss of their Husbands and Parents their children. Tis said about 80 or 90 of the men were English, Scotch and Irish and the rest of Dutch Parentage most born in this country." -- Alma Cramer N
1 CONC otes for which the source appears to by New Jersey Archives -- First Series, Vol. XI, p 4, The Boston News Letter Dec 16 to Dec 24, 1705 (no. 88).
1 CONT
1 CONT "Otto Van Toyle or Van Tayl or Vantyle had been captured and imprisoned in 1699 as one of Capt. Kid's men but released without bail to the alarm of the honest merchant skippers -- New York Colonial Documents IV, pp 551 and 623 -- He now turns up a
1 CONC gain as the commander of a privateer."
1 CONT
1 CONT Alma Cramer notes the following references to Otto Van Tuyl's activities as a privateer and an associate of Captain Kidd:
1 CONT 1.	Documents Relative to the Colonial History of the State of New York, Vol. IV, pp. 551 and 623.
1 CONT 2.	The Pirates "Who's who." by Philip Gosse, 1924, pp 168, 302, and 319.
1 CONT 3.	The pirates of Madagascar," by Herbert Deschamps, 1949, pp 63 and 159.
1 CONT 4.	Piracy was a Business," by Cyrus H. Karraker, pp 80, 102
