0 @N1056@ NOTE
1 CONC William and his brother Thomas probably did not move to Stafford Co. Va until after the 1701 death of their father.  They were both in the Surry Co. Militia in the company of Captain Joseph Sumner in 1701.
1 CONT They were in Stafford Co. by 1723 when William was registered in Overwharton Parish taxables as paying 96 lbs. of tobacco for 400 acres.
1 CONT
1 CONT When William, the older died ca 1739/39 he left land to son William and daughter Mary Peters.  He also left land for the care of Margaret, widow of brother Thomas and their daughter, Margaret.  A Margaret Whitson died in 1745, possibly the widow o
1 CONC f Thomas.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sources Will book M, p 262, Stafford Co. Va 1739.
0 @N1057@ NOTE
1 CONC Wife unknown.  Died testate in Virginia
1 CONT
1 CONT "Court records show him to have been a minor in 1744, with the privilege of selection his own guardian.  An orphan in Virginia was allowed to choose his own guardian at the age of fourteen, and in such instances a boy was declared to be able to ma
1 CONC nage his own estate at the age to eighteen.  Therefore, at the death of his father in 1743, George Yates IV  was more than fourteen years of age but less than eighteen years.  His birth, therefore, occurred sometime between the years of 1728 and 1
1 CONC 730.  He was born, evidently, in or about the year 1728 in Caroline County, Va.
1 CONT "George Yates IV chose, in court on Feb 10, 1743, William Conner to act as his guardian, which was approved by the court.  in 1747, it appears George Yates IV was still a minor but had the right to join his guardian in a court petition.
1 CONT "On April 13, 1758, George Yates was commissioned a Lieutenant of the Colonial Militia of the County.  On Aug. 8, 1765, George Yates was commissioned a Captain of the Militia.
1 CONT "George Yates IV died testate on Caroline County sometime prior to Dec. 11, 1777.  His will was admitted to probate during the court session of March 1778, but the records at that time did not record the names of the executors, but they do state '
1 CONC executrix and executors.'  The natural conclusion is that the executrix was his widow, but the court book failing to record the name we thus lose another opportunity of obtaining the first name of his wife and widow.
1 CONT "From a court order book of March 1778, it is learned that Michael Yates and John Yates are two of the Executors.
1 CONT "At the October term of the court, in 1781, William Yates, orphan son of George Yates IV, 'made choice of Michael Yates for his guardian.'  Also at the same term of court, in October, 1781, it was recorded that 'Benjamin Winn is appointed by the C
1 CONC ourt [as] Guardian of Molly, George, and Warner Yates,  orphans of George Yates, IV dec'd.'" -- Newmann. p. 13
1 CONT
1 CONT LKL wonders what happened to the orphans between 1778 and 1781?  Were they under the care of their mother who then died around 1781?
