1 CONT JAMES JOHNS' REVOLUTIONARY WAR MILITARY SERVICE RECORD:
1 CONT
1 CONT The following is a summary of James Johns' military service during the Ame rican Revolution.  It is based on information found in his military servi ce file kept by the National Archives and Records Administration. The info rmation is limited, al
1 CONC l of it being found on monthly regimental muster a nd payroll sheets.
1 CONT
1 CONT James was drafted into service as a private with the 14th Virginia Regime nt on October 1, 1777.  There is no indication of the place where he was d rafted.  The companies that made up the 14th Virginia were raised from num erous counties in centr
1 CONC al and southern Virginia, ranging from Dinwiddie a nd Prince George in the east to Albemarle in the north, to Botetourt in t he west, and to Pittsylvania and Halifax in the south.  We know that Jam es lived in Buckingham county both before and aft
1 CONC er the war.  Buckingham l ies in the middle of the region from which the 14th was recruited.
1 CONT
1 CONT James' company, consisting of about 100 men, was led by Captain John Overt on.  Overton commanded the company throughout the known term of James' ser vice.
1 CONT
1 CONT James was first listed on regimental pay returns dated January, 1778, wi th the remark that he "Lately joined the 14th Virga Regt. -- never drew p ay or any payroll before".  His pay was 6 and 2/3 dollars per month.
1 CONT
1 CONT The February, 1778, payroll sheet identified the regimental commander as C olonel Lewis.  The muster roll stated that James had enlisted for three ye ars service.
1 CONT
1 CONT In March, 1778, James was with his regiment at Valley Forge, where it h ad been since the previous November.  The 14th had undergone a change of c ommand; Lt. Col. Abraham Buford was now in charge.
1 CONT
1 CONT In April the regiment remained at Valley Forge.  There had been another ch ange in leadership, Colonel William Davies was now commanding.  Davies l ed the 14th throughout the remainder of James Johns' service.
1 CONT
1 CONT The 14th, part of George Washington's army, left Valley Forge in mid Ju ne to pursue the British army, which had just evacuated Philadelphia.
1 CONT
1 CONT By August, 1778, the regiment was posted at White Plains, about 25 miles n orth of New York City.  Washington's army, revived and in high spirits aft er a miserable winter, had followed General Sir Henry Clinton's army acro ss New Jersey, lookin
1 CONC g for a chance to strike it.  On June 28, 1778, the t wo armies collided in a brief, furious battle at Monmouth Court Hous e.  On September 1, the day the August roll was drawn up, James was list ed as "sick, present".  Whatever ailment he suffere
1 CONC d from may have continu ed to bother him because the September roll, made in early October while t he 14th was at West Point, New York, noted that James was "Sick at Fish Ki ll".
1 CONT
1 CONT By October, the regiment was in Middlebrook, New Jersey, and had gone in to winter camp.
1 CONT
1 CONT In December, 1778, because many regiments of the Continental army were bad ly understrength due to battle deaths, wounds, expiration of service, sick ness and desertion, the 14th was redesignated the 10th Virginia.
1 CONT
1 CONT The newly renamed 10th Virginia remained at Middlebrook, New Jersey, throu gh April, 1779.  In May and June they were posted at a place called Smith 's Clove, near present day Monroe, New York, about 40 miles north of New Y ork City.
1 CONT
1 CONT During July, August and September of 1779, the 10th was in Camp Ramapou gh in northern New Jersey.  Ramapough was about 30 miles northeast of Phil adelphia.  The army was guarding the approaches of that city.  In Septembe r, James Johns was liste
1 CONC d as "sick, present"  For the month he was pa id a total of 16 and 2/3 dollars, 10 of which was for subsistence and t he remainder as pay.
1 CONT
1 CONT In October, the regiment had moved to Haverstraw, New York.  In Novemb er it was at Morristown, New Jersey, and had gone into winter camp.
1 CONT
1 CONT Here the records end, even though James had a year more to serve.  What ha ppened to the regimental documents?  Probably this:  In early December t he 10th Virginia was assigned to the Southern Department of the Continent al army and marched sout
1 CONC h to Charleston, South Carolina.  There it had t he great misfortune of being assigned to the Colonial forces under Gener al Benjamin Lincoln.  Thanks to Lincoln's vacillations and incompetence t he Charleston garrison was captured in its entiret
1 CONC y by the British under G eneral Sir Henry Clinton, on May 12, 1780.  This is probably why regiment al records don't appear in James John's file.  They were lost as a resu lt of the capture of the regiment, or destroyed by the British captors.
1 CONT
1 CONT 14th/10th Virginia Regiment
1 CONT Itinerary and Chronology
1 CONT During James Johns' Service
1 CONT January, 1778 -- December, 1779
1 CONT
1 CONT January to June, 1778      Valley Forge, PA
1 CONT
1 CONT June 28             Monmouth Court House, NJ
1 CONT
1 CONT August              White Plains, NY
1 CONT
1 CONT September 1         James sick at Fish Kill, NY
1 CONT
1 CONT October to April, 1779   Middlebrook, NJ
1 CONT
1 CONT May to June         Smith's Clove [Monroe], NY
1 CONT
1 CONT July to September        Camp Ramapough, NJ
1 CONT
1 CONT October             Haverstraw, NY
1 CONT
1 CONT November       Morristown, NJ
1 CONT
1 CONT December       left Morristown NJ for Charleston, SC
1 CONT
1 CONT May 12, 1780        regiment captured at Charleston by British under Cornw allis
1 CONT
1 CONT Many thanks to Larry Murdock, who is a descendant of James Johns and Ma ry Gannaway, for providing the Revolutionary War Service Record as we ll as his transcription of the pension papers for James Johns.
