0 @N1445@ NOTE
1 CONC "YOUNG COUPLE SURPRISES FRIENDS
1 CONT "The marriage of Miss Kathryn Taylor and Othel V. Mitchell on New Year's eve came as a surprise to their many friends in this city and county.  While close friends understood that the couple were engaged they did not know that they intended to b
1 CONC e married at this time.  The wedding ceremony was quietly performed by the Rev. Harold Hunt, pastor of the Bethel Baptist church at his home at LaGrange about eleven o'clock in the evening.  The young people were accompanied by Eugene Taylor, brot
1 CONC her of the bride, and Vernon Brinkley.  Mrs Mitchell is the daughter of Mr. and Mrs. J.B. Taylor of north of this city.  For several years she has been teaching school in Chicago and the marriage occurred while she was home on a Christmas vacation
1 CONC .  Mr. Mitchell is a son of Mr. and Mrs. A. S. Mitchell and until recently was associated with his father in conducting a bus line between this city and Quincy.  It is understood the young couple will make their home in Chicago." -- Palmyra Specta
1 CONC tor, Jan ?, 1931
1 CONT
1 CONT Until, I saw the above clipping, I never knew that Kathryn and Othel had ben married with such haste and secrecy, although, now that I think of it, there were never any wedding pictures in evidence.  I wonder what my grandparents, particularly m
1 CONC y grandmother, thought about their only daughter's depriving them of the chance to give her a big wedding?  	I don't think that the marriage was a great success.  Othel apparently drank excessively on occasion. She was a teatotalling Southern Bapt
1 CONC ist.  He liked hunting, fishing, and being "with the boys."  She liked the symphony, opera, and clothes.  She didn't particularly like to cook.  They never had any children.  She taught throughout her marriage.  Was this because he was sometimes u
1 CONC nemployed or because she liked the luxuries afforded by two incomes? -- LKL
0 @N1446@ NOTE
1 CONC "Couple Wed in Palmyra Church
1 CONT "The marriage of Miss Bonnie Lou Todd, daughter of Mr and Mrs Raymond Todd of Palmyra to Richard Henry Kellenberg, son of Mrs. Anna Kellenberg of Greenfield, Ia., was performed in the First Baptist church Sunday, August 24.  Rev. W.E. Sutterfiel
1 CONC d read the double ring ceremony assisted by Dr. W.E. Davidson of Hannibal.
1 CONT "Preceding the ceremony Mrs. F.G. Carlsted and Mrs W.E. Sutterfield presented a brief recital of organ and piano music.  Mis Janet White, cousin of the bride, lighted the candles and Miss Abbie Todd, sister of the bride sang.
1 CONT "The bride was escorted to the alter by her father.  She was wearing a ballerina length gown of Chantilly lace, styled with strapless bodice and matching long sleeved jacket with Peter Pan collar.  Her fingertip veil of imported French illusion wa
1 CONC s caught to a shirred tiara of matching illusion with pearlized orange blossom trim.  Her bouquet of yellow roses was arranged in a simulated half moon.
1 CONT "Attending the bride were Mrs. B.L. Todd of Liberty, matron of honor, Miss Abbie Todd of Palmyra, maid of honor, Miss Janet White of Candalia [i.e., Vandalia], Ill., Miss Marilyn Olson of Knoxville, Ia., Mrs. Roger Barrett of Iowa City, Ia. brides
1 CONC maids, Miss Mary Mensing of Greenfield, Ia., flower girl, Miss Norma Kellenberg of Greenfield, flower girl, Dennis Kellenberg of Greenfield, ring bearer....
1 CONT "The groom was attended by Paul Kellenberg of Greenfield, Ia., best man, Bill L. Todd of Liberty, head usher, Roger Barrett, Iowa City, Joseph Maas of Kansas City, Kan., Herbert Hoener of Quincy, ushers.
1 CONT "Immediately following the ceremony a reception was held at the Todd home.  Assisting were ...Mrs. Austin White....  Miss Hazel White ... assisted with the gifts....
1 CONT "The bride is a graduate of Palmyra High school and Iowa State College [i.e., University] at Ames where she received a degree in home economics.  Since graduating, she has been a member of the Iowa State College Extension Field staff as county hom
1 CONC e economist at Greenfield.  She is a member of Sigma Kappa social sorority and has accepted a position in the Kansas City public school system for the coming year.
1 CONT "The groom, after serving two years in the navy, was graduated from Iowa State College with a degree in landscape architecture.  He is a member of Tau Sigma Delta honorary fraternity and is at present employed by the firm of Hare and Hare Landscap
1 CONC e Architects in Kansas City...."  Palmura Spectator, Tuesday, Sept 2, 1952
0 @N1447@ NOTE
1 CONC Hale gives date of marriage as Sept 12, 1837.
0 @N1448@ NOTE
1 CONC Undated clipping from the Palmyra Spectator:
1 CONT "MCKAY -- YEAGER -- Married at the residence of the bride's parents in this county, on the 30th ult.. Mr. J.S. McKay of Taylor, Mo. and Miss Ella Yeager.  The best wishes of SPECTATOR follow the young couple."
0 @N1449@ NOTE
1 CONC Is this the Elizabeth Maxwell that was indentured to his father?--LKL
1 CONT
1 CONT Marriage was witnessed by Mary Worley, Nathan Worley, Ann Howell, Lydia Howell, Thomas Vernon, Esther Vernon, Lydia Vernon--Bill Jobe
0 @N1450@ NOTE
1 CONC "Nov. 1, 1730, Margaret McKay, dtr. of Robert and Ann of Milford Hd. Cecil Co. Md. and Joshua Job s. of Andrew and Elizabeth."  -- Nottingham Marriages as quoted by Hunter McKay in The McKays and Allied Families.  Jobe gives the same date but in J
1 CONC obe's Journal, the date is given as 11 Eleventh month 1730.
1 CONT
1 CONT Dee Ann (Shipp) Buck gives the year of marriage as 1731
0 @N1451@ NOTE
1 CONC Undated clipping from the Palmyra Spectator:
1 CONT "TAYLOR -- BLISS --At St. Francesville, Mo. March 9, 1887, Mr. Joshua T. Taylor, of Marion County, and Miss Mattie A. Bibb, of Clark county, Eld W.D., Cave of Kahoka, officiating.
1 CONT Friend Josh has certainly plucked one of Clark's brightest gems, and many are wishing them a happy journey through this world and a bright glory in the next.
0 @N1452@ NOTE
1 CONC Winchester Republican, Thursday, Jan 20, 1831. Marriage
1 CONT "on Wed. last, by the Rev George Reed, Capt. Richard Phelps of Lynchburg to Miss Asbury Ann, daughter of Dr. John B. Tilden of Newtown."
1 CONT
1 CONT J.E.T. Notes that a George Read was husband of Martha Tilden, eldest sister of Asburyanna.
0 @N1453@ NOTE
1 CONC Among the witnesses were Robert McKay and Mary MacKay, which possibly proves a second wife named Mary.
0 @N1454@ NOTE
1 CONC Bill Jobe gives the date as 10 July 1749-- Jobe's journal, p 66.  BUT Jobe, on p 65, says that 22 August 1749 Robert McKay served as a surety for daughter Leah Taylor's bond as administrator of the estate of William Taylor!  LKL thinks she must ha
1 CONC ve been married in the late 1730's when she would have been in her early 20's
0 @N1455@ NOTE
1 CONC Date: Ninth month 24, 1726.  Probable place of marriage according to Bill Jobe  Marriage was at the New Garden Meeting
0 @N1456@ NOTE
1 CONC date of marriage given as 9 March 1834  -- Hunter B. McKay.  The McKay's and Allied Families, p. 235
0 @N1457@ NOTE
1 CONC "57th Wedding Anniversary
1 CONT "It was just fifty-seven years ago today, December 23 since General William A. Compton launched his barque on the unexplored sea of matrimony and rode away with his blushing bride, a daughter of Mr. R. M. Bolen, of the then called Harmony Hollow.
1 CONT "No six cylinder Packard awaited the happy couple to conduct them on flowery beds of ease, but hitched to the horse-track stood two trusty steeds.
1 CONT "On horseback, the wedding trip was made to the home of the groom's father at Overall 9passing through Front Royal to show off, the general blushingly acknowledged) where the newly-weds were royally received.
1 CONT "Locking [sic] back over the long vista of married happiness the General only regrets that he has not two lives to live for, says he, 'I would not change one incident, most especially that pertaining to matrimony.'
1 CONT "There is, perhaps, no more beautiful sight than an old couple who have fought life's battle together, walking peacefully, hand in hand out into the setting sun.
1 CONT "May the General and his better half live long to enjoy the fruits of their labors.
1 CONT "Amen -- C.W.C. in Warren Sentinel -- Clipping in the "Taylor Notebook."   It is not from the Palmyra paper.  It may have been a reprint in another Virginia paper from the Warren [Va.] Sentinel -- undated, but probably 1924
0 @N1458@ NOTE
1 CONC Presumably died young
0 @N1459@ NOTE
1 CONC Abt. 1798 in IGI
0 @N1460@ NOTE
1 CONC Funeral was October 4, 1900.  He was Mayor of Palmyra in 1898.  Educated at LaGrange and Ingleside Colleges. -- Stephens, Dan V.  Stephens Genealogies..., #1004.
0 @N1461@ NOTE
1 CONC "Obituary
1 CONT "Melissa Ellenora Carson, the daughter of William and Aletha Carson, was born June 7, 1843, in Marion county, Mo; was married to John W Mallary at Palmyra, Mo., May 26th, 1864.  To this union were born five children, four daughters and one son, al
1 CONC l of whom survive her.  She died Sept. 22nd, 1896, age 53 years, 3 months and 15 days.  She joined Bethel Baptist church at an early age of eleven years and was baptized by Rev. J.T. Williams.
1 CONT "Seldom has death come more unexpectedly...  For years she had been a constant sufferer...  Just when they felt she was better and would probably be spared to them for years, she was suddenly called home...." -- Palmyra Spectator (?)  This long ob
1 CONC ituary to be found in full in the Taylor scrapbook has been severely cropped here.
0 @N1462@ NOTE
1 CONC She was still living in 1983 when she attended her sister's 93rd birthday party, but dead by the time her brother, Warwick Triplett died in 1989. -- LKL
